\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Set 2 - Vector Autoregression (VAR)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
The primary papers are: Sargent and Sims (Nobel Prize in 2011); Christiano
 and Eichenbaum and Evans (1999); Leeper, Sims, and Zha (1996).
 This set of lecture notes presents a basic overview of vector autogregression,
 and it provides a brief explanation of the role and effects of monetary
 policy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Intuition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Question: What does monetary policy do?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Historical answer:
\series default
 Friedman-Schwartz answered this question by analyzing the sequences of
 output and monetary aggregates.
 The primary method used to perform this analysis is to construct a bi-variate
 regression model 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{t},m_{t})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 represents output and 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{t}$
\end_inset

 represents monetary aggregates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Single Equation Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}=c_{y}+{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{S}}\alpha_{ys}y_{t-s}+{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{S}}\beta_{ys}m_{t-s}+u_{yt}
\]

\end_inset

This model can be estimated by OLS to obtain the estimated coefficients
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{c}_{y},\{\hat{\alpha}\}_{s=1}^{S},\{\hat{\beta}_{ys}\}_{s=1}^{S})$
\end_inset

, and we may argue that 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\hat{\beta}_{ys}\}_{s=1}^{S}$
\end_inset

 represent the effects of the monetary policy.
 However, what is wrong with this method?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Criticism:
\series default
 Endogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Observed movements in 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 may be driven by several factors, which can affect the change in the money
 supply, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta m$
\end_inset

.
 Two possible factors are: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
An unexpected policy change (pure monetary policy shock).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The Fed's systematic response to macroeconomics conditions, i.e., business
 cycles, financial shocks, inflation, current technology, etc.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If this is true, then the coefficients, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\hat{\beta}_{ys}\}_{s=1}^{S}$
\end_inset

, contain the mixtures of these effects.
 We cannot assume that correlation implies causality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For simplicity, lets assume that the monetary policy is described by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
m_{t} & = & c_{m}+{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{S}}\alpha_{ms}y_{t-s}+{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{S}}\beta_{ms}m_{t-s}+u_{ms}\\
m_{t} & = & A_{mt}+u_{ms}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{mt}$
\end_inset

 captures the Fed's systematc response to past information and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{ms}$
\end_inset

 is the disturbance.
 This model can be estimated by OLS to obtain the estimated coefficients
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{c}_{m},\{\hat{\alpha}_{ms}\}_{s=1}^{S},\{\hat{\beta}_{ms}\}_{s=1}^{S})$
\end_inset

 and the residuals 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\hat{u}_{mt}\}_{t=1}^{T}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Interpretation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{mt}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{mt}$
\end_inset

 is closer to the pure monetary policy shock because at least effects of
 past 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{s}$
\end_inset

 and past 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{s}$
\end_inset

 are washed away.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
It's not pure monetary policy shock because there are contemporaneous technology
 shocks, financial shocks, etc.
 Most likely, the Fed will react to these occurences as well.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{mt}=e_{mt}+\phi e_{yt}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{mt}$
\end_inset

 represents monetary policy shock and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{yt}$
\end_inset

 represents the combinations of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

other shocks.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We observe 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{mt}$
\end_inset

, but what we want to know is 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{mt}$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have to wash 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{yt}$
\end_inset

 away.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
VAR
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we construct a bi-variate regression model 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{t},m_{t})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 represents output and 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{t}$
\end_inset

 represents monetary aggregates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
y_{t}\\
m_{t}
\end{array}\right) & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
c_{y}\\
c_{m}
\end{array}\right)+{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{S}}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
\alpha_{ys} & \beta_{ys}\\
\alpha_{ms} & \beta_{ms}
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{c}
y_{t-s}\\
m_{t-s}
\end{array}\right)+\left(\begin{array}{c}
u_{yt}\\
u_{mt}
\end{array}\right)\\
u_{mt} & = & e_{mt}+\phi e_{yt}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{mt}$
\end_inset

 is monetary policy shock and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{yt}$
\end_inset

 represents the other shocks.
 What about 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{yt}$
\end_inset

? Maybe 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{yt}$
\end_inset

 does not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{mt}$
\end_inset

 (the monetary policy has effects on the real economy with time lag).
 If we accept this, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{yt}=e_{yt}$
\end_inset

.
 Now, we find that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
e_{mt} & = & u_{mt}-\phi e_{yt}\\
 & = & u_{mt}-\phi u_{yt}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

 So, we can back out 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pure
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 monetary policy shock.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{e}_{mt}=\hat{u}_{mt}-\phi\hat{u}_{yt}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Structural VAR (Hamilton Ch.
 11)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}=c+{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{S}}A_{s}y_{t-s}+u_{t}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{s}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times K$
\end_inset

 vector of coefficients, and all the variables are 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We view elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 as a mixture of structural shocks.
 An economic interpretation of a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

structural shock
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is a pure shock that only affects one part of the economy.
 In particular, we assume
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{t}=C\epsilon_{t}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}:K\times1$
\end_inset

 are shocks in VAR, 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{t}:K\times1$
\end_inset

 are structural shocks (unobserved; independent of monetary policy), and
 
\begin_inset Formula $C:K\times K$
\end_inset

 is an unknown matrix.
 We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(\epsilon_{t})=I_{K}$
\end_inset

, i.e., all the structrual shocks are uncorrelated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Once we recover 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

, while combining it with the the residuals from the VAR regression 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{u}_{t})$
\end_inset

, we can obtain the full history of structural shocks.
 Some useful information to know about 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 is the moment restriction
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Cov}(u_{t})=CC^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset

This covariance matrix gives us a system of 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{K(K+1)}{2}$
\end_inset

 equations and 
\begin_inset Formula $K^{2}$
\end_inset

 unknowns.
 We need 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{K(K-1)}{2}$
\end_inset

 additional equations to pin down 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

.
 This is the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Identification Assumption.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 One particularly common assumption is recursive identification, which assumes
 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 is lower triangular
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
x &  & 0\\
 & \ddots\\
x &  & x
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

An innovation to the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-th variable does not have contemporaneous effects on the 1st 
\begin_inset Formula $(k-1)$
\end_inset

-th variables.
 An innovation to the 
\begin_inset Formula $(k-1)$
\end_inset

-th variables does not have contemporaneous effects on the 1st 
\begin_inset Formula $(K-2)$
\end_inset

-th variables, and so on.
 We now have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(u_{t})=CC^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(\nicefrac{K(K+1)}{2}\ \mbox{equations})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 is lower triangular 
\begin_inset Formula $(\nicefrac{K(K-1)}{2}\ \mbox{equations})$
\end_inset

.
 So, there are now 
\begin_inset Formula $K^{2}$
\end_inset

 equations and 
\begin_inset Formula $K^{2}$
\end_inset

 unknowns.
 Therefore, it seems that we can find 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

.
 How can we do this? Using Cholesky decomposition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Advancement in the Literature
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Identification assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Block recurisve identifcation (Christiano, Eichenbaum, Evans (1999))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
x_{1t}\\
s_{t}\\
x_{2t}
\end{array}\right)\begin{array}{c}
k_{1}\times1\\
\mbox{policy instrument}\\
k_{2}\times1
\end{array}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Long-run restriction (Blanchard and Quah (1989); Gali (?))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Blanchard and Quah study bi-variate VAR.
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\Delta Y_{t},u_{t})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}$
\end_inset

 is GNP and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 is unemployment.
 Their identification assumption relies on 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the demand shock that does not have permanent effect on GNP.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Factor-Augmented Vector Regression (FAVAR) (Bernake and Boivin (2003); Bernake,
 Boivin, and Eliasz (2005))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is a spectrum from small VAR to large VAR.
 Small VAR conserves the degrees of freedom, but it is unlikely to span
 the monetary authority's information set.
 Large VAR is more realistic, but it needs more identification assumptions
 and it has lower estimation power.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The setup for large VAR is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(\begin{array}{c}
F_{t}\\
Y_{t}
\end{array}\right)=c+{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{S}}A_{s}\left(\begin{array}{c}
F_{t-s}\\
Y_{t-s}
\end{array}\right)+u_{t}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $M\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of observed economic variables and 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

factors.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{t}$
\end_inset

 is extracted form a large number of time series 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}:N\times1$
\end_inset

 by applying principle component analysis 
\emph on
(Stock and Watson (2002), JBES)
\emph default
.
\end_layout

